Sever's Disease (Calcaneal Apophysitis)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Adolescent heel pain, Positive calcaneal squeeze test, Exacerbation with jumping/cleats

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-054
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of bilateral heel pain in a child following a weekend sporting tournament, causing an inability to bear weight on the heels.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient (11 years old) and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified an adolescent who developed agonizing heel pain and an acute inability to walk normally (severe toe-walking) following a soccer tournament. Urgent assessment was strictly required to rule out an acute calcaneal stress fracture, Achilles tendon rupture, or occult bone infection, and to provide immediate mechanical offloading and reassurance to highly distressed parents prior to daytime pediatric/sports review.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The backs of my heels are burning and throbbing so badly I can't put my feet flat on the floor; I have to walk on my tiptoes."
History of Present Illness: An 11-year-old patient, currently undergoing a rapid growth spurt, presents with an acute, severe flare of bilateral posterior heel pain. Timeline: Insidious onset of mild heel discomfort over the past 2 months. This weekend, the patient participated in a rigorous 2-day soccer tournament wearing cleated footwear. Following the final game today, the pain became significantly exacerbated, rendering the patient unable to walk flat-footed. The patient exhibits an extreme antalgic gait, employing compensatory toe-walking to entirely offload the symptomatic heels. The pain is described as a sharp, bruising ache.
Functional Status: Ambulation is severely impaired; patient is strictly toe-walking and requires physical support for balance.
Social/Safety Concerns: High parental anxiety regarding potential permanent bone or tendon damage.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 112/70 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 40 Kg
General: Alert, in visible discomfort, sitting with both feet dangling, refusing to let heels touch the floor.
Physical Exam: Bilateral heels reveal mild localized warmth and subtle soft tissue swelling strictly confined over the posterior calcaneal insertion. Crucially, there is a complete absence of focal Achilles tendon thickening, nodularity, or palpable mid-substance defects (Thompson squeeze test is negative, ruling out tendon rupture). Exquisite, severe pain is elicited during a positive calcaneal squeeze test (simultaneously compressing the medial and lateral aspects of the calcaneal tuberosity). Measurable, severe tightness of the gastrocnemius-soleus complex is noted, restricting passive ankle dorsiflexion. Consistent pain provocation during functional loading (patient refuses to perform a single-leg heel raise). No systemic fevers or tracking erythema.
Environmental Observations: Cleated soccer boots visible in the hallway, confirming the specific mechanical trigger (cleats concentrate force on the heel).
Medication Reconciliation: Parents administered Paracetamol 500mg with minimal relief of the acute mechanical tension pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute severe exacerbation of Sever's Disease (Calcaneal Apophysitis) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Calcaneal Stress Fracture - Difficult to rule out entirely without an X-ray, but the bilateral, symmetric presentation following a specific sporting tournament in a rapidly growing 11-year-old overwhelmingly favors apophysitis.

Achilles Tendinopathy/Rupture - Ruled out clinically by the normal, thin, non-tender mid-substance of the Achilles tendon and the pain being strictly localized to the bony insertion (apophysis) and lateral squeeze.

Osteomyelitis (Calcaneus) - Ruled out by the bilateral presentation, clear mechanical athletic trigger, and absence of systemic fever or intense, unilateral localized heat/erythema.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The pediatric patient presented with a textbook, severe flare of calcaneal apophysitis (Sever's disease) following rigorous athletic loading in cleats. The critical clinical imperative was to differentiate this benign, mechanically driven growth plate inflammation from an acute structural fracture or deep bone infection. Clinical examination definitively localized the pathology to the calcaneal apophysis (positive bilateral squeeze test) and confirmed the intact status of the Achilles tendon. The severe pain and obligatory toe-walking caused extreme parental distress. Postponing evaluation would have subjected the child to unmanaged severe pain and left the parents highly anxious. Urgent after-hours assessment provided crucial diagnostic reassurance, excluded surgical emergencies, and initiated aggressive conservative pain and biomechanical load management.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a short course of oral Ibuprofen (weight-based dosing) with food for 3-5 days to aggressively target the localized apophyseal inflammation. Advised absolute cessation of all running, jumping, and cleated sports immediately.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Strictly apply an ice pack wrapped in a towel directly to the back of the heels for 15 minutes after any necessary walking. Consequence: Immediate suppression of the local inflammatory chemical response, drastically reducing the severe, burning pain and visibly shrinking the soft tissue swelling (positive reinforcement).
Action: Insert simple, over-the-counter gel heel raises (lifts) into all daily footwear starting tomorrow. Consequence: The slight lift immediately shortens the calf muscle, taking the mechanical tension directly off the fragile, inflamed growth plate and drastically reducing the sharp pain when walking flat-footed (negative reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Physiotherapy or Podiatry referral highly recommended for calf stretching protocols and orthotic management once the acute pain settles.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks. Return to sport is strictly contraindicated until completely pain-free with daily activities and cleared by a professional.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the child develops a high fever, if the heel becomes bright red and intensely hot, or if they suddenly cannot push their foot down against resistance at all.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a clinical assessment of growth plate inflammation. X-rays are generally unnecessary as they will show a fragmented apophysis normally in this age group, but may be arranged by the GP to rule out a stress fracture if recovery is delayed. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: This condition is entirely self-limiting and will completely resolve without permanent damage once the heel growth plate fuses (around age 14-15). Management relies entirely on activity modification and calf flexibility.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if your child develops a fever and the heel becomes bright red and hot to the touch, or if the pain continues to worsen despite complete rest.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: During a rapid growth spurt, the leg bones grow faster than the calf muscles. Your child's tight calf muscles are pulling aggressively on the relatively soft growth plate at the back of the heel. The cleated shoes and jumping today caused severe microscopic tearing and inflammation at that attachment site. The ice and anti-inflammatories will calm the acute fire, but inserting heel lifts is the mechanical cure.
Actionable Steps: Absolutely no soccer or running. Use ice directly on the heels tonight to stop the throbbing pain. Tomorrow, purchase gel heel lifts for their shoes; this acts like a physical slack-line for the tight calf muscle, instantly dropping the pain.

